<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adolphe, Grand Duke of Luxembourg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1817">1817</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Adolphe of Nassau, Adolphe, Duke of Nassau, Adolphe of Nassau-Weilburg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adolphe was the last Duke of Nassau, from 1839 to 1866, and Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> from 1890 to 1905. He lost his duchy when he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed it in 1866. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III of the Netherlands</a> died without a male heir in 1890, Adolphe inherited Luxembourg under the Nassau family pact, ending its personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolphe-grand-duke-of-luxembourg.jpg" alt="Adolphe, Grand Duke of Luxembourg" width="269" height="372" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adolfluxembourg1817-6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Adolphe was born on 24 July 1817, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-nassau">William, Duke of Nassau</a>, and Princess Louise of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hildburghausen">Hildburghausen</a>. He became Duke of Nassau in August 1839 at the age of 22, and in 1841 took up residence in the newly built Stadtschloss at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wiesbaden">Wiesbaden</a>, by then the capital of the duchy. On 4 March 1848 he consented to the population's nine Demands of the Nassauers, but a few years later revoked his liberal views and took a strongly conservative course. He was nevertheless seen as a popular ruler. In 1844 he married Grand Duchess Elizabeth Mikhailovna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, who died within a year, and he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> of Saint Elizabeth in her memory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1839">Treaty of London</a> of 1839 had made the grand duchy independent but in personal union with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. Luxembourg followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salic-law">Salic law</a>, which barred women from succession, so when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III of the Netherlands</a> died in 1890 his daughter Wilhelmina took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> crown while Luxembourg passed to Adolphe under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nassau-family-pact">Nassau Family Pact</a>. He was 73 and knew little of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourgish">Luxembourgish</a> politics. He left day-to-day government to Prime Minister Paul Eyschen, who had held office since 1888, which set a convention that the monarch stay out of the politics of the day. In 1902 he named his son William Lieutenant-Representative.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Adolphe died in 1905 at his summer home, Schloss Hohenburg in Lenggries, and was succeeded by his son William IV. In 1953 he was buried in the crypt of the church at Schloss Weilburg. With his second wife, Princess Adelheid-Marie of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dessau">Dessau</a>, whom he married in 1851, he had five children, of whom two lived beyond eighteen. In 1842, at his castle at Biebrich, the Adelsverein, a society for German immigrants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, was organised under his protection, and its Nassau Plantation in Fayette County, Texas, was named after him. In 1892 he gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> title of Count of Wisborg to his Swedish nephew Oscar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adolphe, Grand Duke of Luxembourg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When William III died in 1890 leaving only a daughter, Wilhelmina, the Nassau Family Pact of 1783 passed Luxembourg to the other branch of the House of Nassau, and <b>Adolphe</b>, the former Duke of Nassau, became grand duke, giving the country its own dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-luxembourg">Grand Duchy of Luxembourg · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke <b>Adolphe</b> sided with Austria in 1866, and Prussia annexed Nassau into the Province of Hesse-Nassau.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nassau">Duchy of Nassau</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biebrich-palace">Biebrich Palace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Schloss Hohenburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.04,8.23&amp;year=1874" aria-label="Biebrich Palace on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4739 423 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Adolphe, Grand Duke of Luxembourg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5189 693h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5189 693h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.04,8.23&amp;year=1874">What was here in 1874? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
